::: {.doc-subject}

Add an edge between two existing nodes in the graph.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L1767-L1981){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.add_edge(
    source,
    target=None,
    rel=None,
    weight=None,
    auto_create_nodes=False,
    **attributes,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Add edge](../assets/graphs/add-edge.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Before: four nodes joined by three edges, C to A to B to D. After: a new edge between the existing nodes C and D, in the accent color, closes the cycle; no nodes are added." width="100%"}

Edges connect existing nodes and can optionally carry weights and attributes. Both source
and target nodes must already exist in the graph. You can reference nodes by their integer
IDs or string labels (if labels are unique).

Each call creates a new edge with its own auto-assigned edge ID, even if an edge between
the same two nodes already exists, so calling it twice for the same pair produces parallel
edges (a multigraph). Self-loops (`source` equal to `target`) are also allowed. Use
`remove_duplicate_edges()`, `remove_selfloops()` or `simplify()` afterward if you need a
simple graph. In a directed graph the edge points from `source=` to `target=`; in an
undirected graph the order of the two endpoints doesn't matter.

The graph is modified in place and the call is recorded in the step log (see `steps`).
For adding many edges at once, `add_edges()` is more concise, and `add_edge_by_id()` is a
strict variant that only accepts integer IDs.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[source]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str, rust.Edge]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The source node identifier: an integer is treated as a node ID and a string as a
    node label. In a directed graph this is the node the edge starts from.

<code>[target]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[Union[int, str]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   The target node identifier (ID or label, resolved the same way as `source=`). In a
    directed graph this is the node the edge points to. Although it has a default of
    `None`, a target is required for normal use.

<code>[rel]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional string relationship type for the edge (e.g., `"friend"`, `"follows"`,
    `"manages"`). This is a first-class attribute that always appears as the fourth column
    in edge DataFrames (null when not set). Default is `None`.

<code>[weight]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[float]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional numeric edge weight, such as a cost, distance or strength. The value is
    stored on the edge whenever it is given (and shown in a `weight` column of the edge
    DataFrame), independently of whether the graph was created with `weighted=True`.
    Default is `None` (no weight).

<code>[auto_create_nodes]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[False]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   If `True`, any string label that doesn't match an existing node causes a new node with
    that label to be created first. Integer IDs are never auto-created. If `False`
    (default), all referenced nodes must already exist.

<code>[**attributes]{.doc-parameter-name}</code>

:   Arbitrary key-value pairs to store as edge attributes (e.g., `since=2020`). Each key
    becomes a column in the edge DataFrame.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Returns self to enable method chaining.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If source or target node doesn't exist in the graph, or if a label cannot be resolved to
    a node ID (and `auto_create_nodes=` is `False`).
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~add_edges`) 

:   Add many edges at once.

[](`~add_edge_by_id`) 

:   Add an edge between node IDs.

[](`~delete_edge`) 

:   Remove an edge.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

If labels are unique, it's usually simplest to reference nodes by label. Since
`add_edge()` returns `self`, the whole graph can be assembled in one chain, and IDs and
labels can be mixed freely:

::: {#333b8479 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = (
    ec.Graph()
    .add_node(label="Alice")
    .add_node(label="Bob")
    .add_node(label="Carol")
    .add_edge(source="Alice", target="Bob")
    .add_edge(source=2, target="Carol")  # node ID 2 is Bob
)

graph.get_edges_str_repr(use_labels=True)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
['Alice--Bob', 'Bob--Carol']
```
:::
:::


`weight=` stores a numeric cost or strength, `rel=` labels the kind of relationship the
edge represents, and any other keyword arguments become additional edge attributes. Each
ends up in its own column of the edge DataFrame:

::: {#d23ae316 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
graph = (
    ec.Graph(weighted=True)
    .add_node(label="Alice")
    .add_node(label="Bob")
    .add_edge(
        source="Alice", target="Bob",
        rel="friend", weight=0.75, since=2020,
    )
)

graph.get_edge_df()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```{=html}
<div><style>
.dataframe > thead > tr,
.dataframe > tbody > tr {
  text-align: right;
  white-space: pre-wrap;
}
</style>
<small>shape: (1, 6)</small><table border="1" class="dataframe"><thead><tr><th>id</th><th>source</th><th>target</th><th>rel</th><th>since</th><th>weight</th></tr><tr><td>i64</td><td>i64</td><td>i64</td><td>str</td><td>i64</td><td>f64</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>&quot;friend&quot;</td><td>2020</td><td>0.75</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
```
:::
:::


Adding the same pair twice doesn't overwrite the first edge; it creates a parallel edge
with its own ID:

::: {#4456e43b .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
graph = (
    ec.Graph()
    .add_nodes(["A", "B"])
    .add_edge("A", "B", rel="email")
    .add_edge("A", "B", rel="phone")
)

graph.get_edge_df()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}
```{=html}
<div><style>
.dataframe > thead > tr,
.dataframe > tbody > tr {
  text-align: right;
  white-space: pre-wrap;
}
</style>
<small>shape: (2, 4)</small><table border="1" class="dataframe"><thead><tr><th>id</th><th>source</th><th>target</th><th>rel</th></tr><tr><td>i64</td><td>i64</td><td>i64</td><td>str</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>&quot;email&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>&quot;phone&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
```
:::
:::


### Payments as they arrive

A bank records payments as they arrive. With `auto_create_nodes=True`, an account that
hasn't been seen before becomes a new node, so the graph grows from the payments alone:

::: {#db3e504b .cell execution_count=4}
``` {.python .cell-code}
incoming = [
    ("Ana", "Ben", 9500, "2026-03-10"),
    ("Ben", "Cruz", 9400, "2026-03-11"),
    ("Cruz", "Ana", 9300, "2026-03-12"),
    ("Cruz", "Ivy", 3000, "2026-03-13"),
    ("Ivy", "Jo", 2950, "2026-03-14"),
    ("Jo", "Kai", 2900, "2026-03-15"),
]

bank = ec.Graph(directed=True, weighted=True)
for payer, payee, amount, date in incoming:
    bank.add_edge(
        payer, payee, weight=amount, date=date, auto_create_nodes=True
    )

bank.count_nodes(), bank.count_edges()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}
```
(6, 6)
```
:::
:::


Each new account and each payment is a step in the graph's log, so `play()` shows them
arriving in order. The third payment closes a circle from Ana to Ben to Cruz and back,
and the money then leaves through Cruz and is passed along from Ivy to Jo to Kai, 50 less
each time:

::: {#62deee00 .cell execution_count=5}
``` {.python .cell-code}
bank.play(seed=2)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=5}
